.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_MSG_COUNT 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_msg_count \- get the current message count of a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

size_t ccol_circq_msg_count(ccol_circular_queue *cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function gives the number of messages that the queue holds at the
time of the call. It reads that number under the lock of the queue, so
the value is a consistent snapshot and not a torn read.

The value is only a snapshot: another thread can send or receive the
instant that the function unlocks the queue, so the value describes a
moment that has already passed when the function returns it. Use the
value for reports and for a drain loop. To learn whether a queue has
room or holds a message, use ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or
ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() instead, because each of those calls gives
the answer and does the action in one step that nothing can break
apart.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue to ask
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The function gives the number of messages that the queue holds, or
ccol_invalid_size ((size_t)-1) when cq is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The count is a thread-safe snapshot.
Zero is an ordinary result that means the queue is empty; only
ccol_invalid_size shows an error.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_chan_msg_count (3)
